drm/xe: don't back up buffers exported through dma-buf

Under machine-wide memory reclaim, the bo shrinker backs up
non-purgeable buffers to shmem, including those a client has exported
through dma-buf for the compositor to sample: its windows. The next
exec that uses such a buffer finds it backed up and restores it from
xe_vm_validate_rebind, under drm_gpuvm_exec_lock, with the buffer's
reservation held. On an integrated GPU with write-combined buffers the
restore sets each page's caching again, and set_pages_array_wc()
flushes every CPU's TLB per chunk. The compositor's own commit locks
the same buffer and waits.

On a Panther Lake laptop under a build, a compositor's main thread was
seen waiting 57 to 220 ms on such locks, held by the clients whose
windows it showed, each in ttm_tt_restore() and mostly in cpa_flush(),
and it missed frames for as long. Raising the non-purgeable shrinker's
seeks to its maximum didn't stop it: whenever reclaim runs, shared
buffers go with the rest.

Skip a non-purgeable buffer while it's exported. Purgeable buffers are
still purged, since their owner has said it can recreate them. Buffers
imported from another device are sg buffers, which the shrinker already
skips for want of TTM_TT_FLAG_EXTERNAL_MAPPABLE.

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/xe/xe_bo.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/xe/xe_bo.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/xe/xe_bo.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/xe/xe_bo.c
@@ -1396,6 +1396,18 @@
 		goto out_unref;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * A buffer exported through dma-buf is most often a client's window,
+	 * which the compositor samples on every frame. Backing it up leaves
+	 * the next submission that uses it to read it back with the
+	 * reservation held, and the compositor stalls on that lock. Leave it
+	 * resident while it is exported.
+	 */
+	if (bo->base.dma_buf) {
+		lret = -EBUSY;
+		goto out_unref;
+	}
+
 	/* System CCS needs gpu copy when moving PL_TT -> PL_SYSTEM */
 	needs_rpm = (!IS_DGFX(xe) && bo->resource->mem_type != XE_PL_SYSTEM &&
 		     xe_bo_needs_ccs_pages(xe_bo));
